before:
With score.h and score.c from the visible definitions below, compile gcc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Werror -c main.c score.c, then link gcc main.o score.o -o app. -c stops before linking. Omitting score.o at link time leaves the definition unresolved. A real incremental build must also rebuild affected objects when included headers change.
```c
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

/* score.c */
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }
```

Complete local C example; the app itself does not execute it:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "score.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", score(4));
    return 0;
}
```

after:
Save each part in the named file. Together they form one example.
```c
// File: score.h
#ifndef SCORE_H
#define SCORE_H
int score(int value);
#endif

// File: score.c
#include "score.h"
int score(int value) { return value * 2; }

// File: main.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include "score.h"

int main(void) {
    printf("%d\n", score(4));
    return 0;
}
```
